1
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:06,580
So, Bernadette, how goes the hunt for bridesmaid dresses?

2
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:07,880
Well, if you don't mind looking like

3
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:09,850
an orange traffic cone, great.

4
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:13,960
Girlfriends, I have the answer to our dress problems.

5
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:14,880
Really?

6
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:18,130
12 years ago, my cousin Irene and her entire family

7
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:20,310
died in a horrific carbon monoxide accident

8
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,010
the night before her wedding.

9
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:24,800
That's horrible.

10
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:26,360
Yes and no.

11
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:29,530
All those bridesmaids dresses remain unused

12
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:31,690
and available to us for free.

13
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:35,630
So it seems that cloud of odorless deadly gas

14
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,300
had a silver lining after all.

15
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:38,790
Check it out,

16
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:39,920
still in the bags.

17
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:41,520
The gowns, not the bridesmaids.

18
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:45,850
I don't know.

19
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,580
Dead people's dresses?

20
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:50,880
Yeah, and cap sleeve...

21
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:54,030
Oh, I was hoping you wouldn't notice.

22
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,990
Irene was always a slave to a good bargain when it came to clothes,

23
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,870
and sadly as it turned out, space heaters.

24
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,090
Ladies, please.

25
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:06,640
These four walls once housed an intellectual salon

26
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:10,120
where the mind received nourishment as well as the stomach.

27
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:12,420
But through no one's fault,

28
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:13,790
Penny,

29
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:18,400
the quality of dinner conversation in this apartment has declined.

30
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:21,400
And again, I'm looking at no one in particular,

31
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:22,510
Penny.

32
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:24,780
Fine.

33
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:26,830
What would you like to talk about, Sheldon?

34
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,960
What would you like to talk about, Sheldon?

35
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:30,810
Why do you hate us?

36
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,980
I've prepared a number of topics that should appeal to

37
00:01:34,030 --> 00:01:37,710
both the advanced and novice conversationalists.

38
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:39,630
Okay, that time you looked at me.

39
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:41,710
Who didn't?

40
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:43,210
Your skin is like alabaster.

41
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:45,120
Do you even have pores?

42
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:48,440
Topic one:

43
00:01:48,490 --> 00:01:50,760
"Faster-than-light particles at CERN--

44
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:52,540
paradigm-shifting discovery

45
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:56,520
or another Swiss export as full of holes as their cheese?"

46
00:01:56,570 --> 00:01:57,970
And converse.

47
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:00,390
All right, who wants to go

48
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:02,190
to my apartment and look at bridal magazines?

49
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:03,220
Oh, me.

50
00:02:03,270 --> 00:02:06,100
Through no one's fault, Sheldon, we're leaving.

51
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:08,630
Wait for moi.

52
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:10,420
You're leaving?

53
00:02:10,470 --> 00:02:14,230
Sheldon, sometimes you forget, I'm a lady.

54
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,030
and with that, comes with an oestrogen fuel need to page through

55
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,100
thick glossy magazines that make me hate my body.

56
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:25,350
New topice: women.

57
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,960
Delightfully mysterious or bat-crap crazy?

58
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,190
Totally.

59
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:32,730
What's wrong with cap sleeves?

60
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,230
If you have the right figure for it, they're adorable.

61
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,770
Hey, Amy, what brings you to our neck of the woods?

62
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,420
Your neurology department loaned me a culture of prions

63
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:11,390
for my research on bovine spongiform encephalopathy.

64
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:18,820
She popped by to borrow a cup of mad cow disease.

65
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,530
It's hard to make degenerative brain maladies hilarious,

66
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:25,680
and yet somehow you do it.

67
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:29,070
That's fun to have in a lunchroom.

68
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:31,570
The real fun starts when you get to pick the rat

69
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:33,050
you're going to feed it to, and maybe you choose

70
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,290
the beady-eyed little mother who's been biting you all week.

71
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:38,980
Please, we're eating.

72
00:03:39,030 --> 00:03:40,560
Can we get that off the table

73
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:41,710
and change the subject?

74
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:42,430
Can we?

75
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,590
Stand back...

76
00:03:44,510 --> 00:03:48,380
while I turn this conversation into a "Conver-sensation."

77
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:51,270
This time, it's your fault.

78
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:54,910
I have 100 alphabetized topics from

79
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,020
"Artichoke-- come on, people, it's just a giant thistle,"

80
00:03:58,070 --> 00:04:02,180
to "Zzz-- the onamona-poetry of sleep."

81
00:04:04,030 --> 00:04:07,810
Amy, how long would it take for that mad cow disease to kill me?

82
00:04:08,970 --> 00:04:10,770
I don't know, four or five years.

83
00:04:11,710 --> 00:04:13,420
No, it's not gonna do it.

84
00:04:17,250 --> 00:04:20,220
Oy. Bernadette keeps texting me pictures

85
00:04:20,270 --> 00:04:23,130
of Penny in bridesmaid dresses.

86
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:24,560
They're out shopping right now?

87
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:25,490
Yeah.

88
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:26,810
Just the two of them?

89
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:28,310
I guess.

90
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:30,880
That's cool, that's cool.

91
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:34,260
Why are they asking me about this stuff?

92
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:38,260
What guy knows what a sweetheart neckline is?

93
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,960
Leonard's coming down the home stretch!

94
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:56,670
Come on, horsey, you can do this!

95
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,540
Damn. Come on, thigh muscles, you can do this!

96
00:05:03,710 --> 00:05:05,900
Yes! First place.

97
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:10,890
I would have been a great jockey if I weren't too tall.

98
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:13,920
and scared of horses.

99
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,020
Sheldon, you're up.

100
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:16,820
What?

101
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:17,730
Come on, it's your turn.

102
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:19,550
We said we'd get more fresh air.

103
00:05:21,630 --> 00:05:23,790
Sorry. I'm a little distracted.

104
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:25,730
I can't seem to get in touch with Amy.

105
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:27,700
I tried e-mail, video chat,

106
00:05:27,750 --> 00:05:30,010
tweeting her, posting on her Facebook wall,

107
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:31,610
texting her, nothing.

108
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:33,970
Did you try calling her on the telephone?

109
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:36,900
The telephone.

110
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:42,690
You know, Leonard, in your own simple way,

111
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:45,090
you may be the wisest of us all.

112
00:05:46,030 --> 00:05:47,460
Voice mail.

113
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:50,680
Curiouser and curiouser.

114
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:53,230
If you're worried, we can go over there

115
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:54,510
and see if she's all right.

116
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:55,650
Okay.

117
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:57,450
You know, I heard in the news

118
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:00,620
a bobcat has been spotted in her neighborhood.

119
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:03,920
I don't think Amy was eaten by a bobcat.

120
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:06,780
Who thinks Amy was eaten by a bobcat?

121
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:09,020
You do?

122
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:13,050
Leonard, I was just mentioning an interesting local news item.

123
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:16,570
Now, thanks to you, I'm worried Amy's been eaten by a bobcat.

124
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:18,360
Forget about the bobcat.

125
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:20,860
How can I? You won't stop talking about it!

126
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:28,300
*Everybody hurts*

127
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:31,080
*Sometimes*

128
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:34,150
*Everybody cries*

129
00:06:38,170 --> 00:06:42,390
*Everybody hurts*

130
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:44,540
*Sometimes.*

131
00:06:46,130 --> 00:06:47,170
She sounds weepy.

132
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:48,870
I don't like weepy. Let's go.

133
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:51,140
She's your friend.

134
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:52,280
Step up.

135
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:56,430
Amy. Amy. Amy.

136
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:57,780
Bye.

137
00:06:57,830 --> 00:06:58,950
Where are you going?

138
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,660
I'm single, I don't need this crap.

139
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:05,200
Sheldon, what are you doing here?

140
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:06,440
You didn't respond to

141
00:07:06,490 --> 00:07:08,500
any of my electronic communications.

142
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:10,880
I wanted to be alone.

143
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:14,940
Would you like to talk about it?

144
00:07:14,990 --> 00:07:19,940
And keep in mind that "No" is a perfectly viable answer.

145
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:23,310
Sheldon, my world is crumbling around me.

146
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:25,310
Point of order.

147
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:28,790
As you're in distress, it would be customary

148
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:31,100
for me to offer you a hot beverage.

149
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:33,750
But I'm a guest in your home,

150
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:37,600
so it would be customary for you to offer me a beverage.

151
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:42,010
How do you want to proceed vis-à-vis beverages?

152
00:07:42,290 --> 00:07:43,390
It doesn't matter.

153
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,170
Nothing matters.

154
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:51,230
If you'd like to take your mind off what's troubling you,

155
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:55,320
uh, word on the street is a bobcat has been spotted.

156
00:07:56,530 --> 00:07:58,300
Penny and Bernadette went shopping

157
00:07:58,350 --> 00:08:00,560
for bridesmaids dresses without me.

158
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,790
And that made you feel...

159
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:05,520
sad?

160
00:08:06,370 --> 00:08:07,270
Yes.

161
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,520
I knew it.

162
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:11,130
Just keep going.

163
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:12,910
I guess I'm good at this.

164
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:17,150
It's just, I thought they liked hanging out with me,

165
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,620
but I guess I was fooling myself.

166
00:08:20,190 --> 00:08:22,350
When they were over here,

167
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,040
did you fail to offer them a beverage?

168
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:30,660
'Cause I can see how that could stick in someone's craw.

169
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:34,630
Sheldon, I'm going to ask you something,

170
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:36,660
and I'd like you to keep an open mind.

171
00:08:36,710 --> 00:08:38,100
Always.

172
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:40,850
At this moment, I find myself

173
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:43,290
craving human intimacy and physical contact.

174
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:44,790
Oh, boy.

175
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:52,730
You know ours is a relationship of the mind.

176
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:54,630
Proposal.

177
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:56,670
One wild night of torrid lovemaking

178
00:08:56,720 --> 00:08:59,290
that soothes my soul and inflames my loins.

179
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:06,070
Counterproposal.

180
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:09,880
I will gently stroke your head

181
00:09:09,930 --> 00:09:13,490
and repeat, "aw, who's a good Amy""

182
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,290
How about this?

183
00:09:18,770 --> 00:09:21,530
French kissing-- seven minutes in heaven

184
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:24,170
culminating in second base.

185
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:30,220
Neck massage...

186
00:09:32,050 --> 00:09:34,670
...then you get me that beverage.

187
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:42,890
We cuddle. Final offer.

188
00:09:44,930 --> 00:09:45,950
Very well.

189
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:54,330
Oh, boy.

190
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,720
I'm just saying, second base is right there.

191
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:30,020
Mm, I'm thinking about adopting some quirky affectation,

192
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:32,150
like a pipe or a monocle

193
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:34,250
or a handlebar mustache.

194
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:37,600
For all those girls out there

195
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:40,390
looking for the Indian Monopoly man?

196
00:10:44,090 --> 00:10:45,260
This is not a safe place.

197
00:10:45,310 --> 00:10:46,990
You can't share anything here.

198
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,150
The two of you need to get your women in line!

199
00:10:55,460 --> 00:10:56,220
What?

200
00:10:56,270 --> 00:10:58,340
Last night I was strong-armed

201
00:10:58,390 --> 00:10:59,650
into an evening of harp music

202
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,150
and spooning with an emotional Amy Farrah Fowler.

203
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:03,310
This on a night

204
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:05,350
that I had originally designated for solving

205
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:07,590
the space-time geometry in higher-spin gravity

206
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:09,500
and building my Lego Death Star.

207
00:11:11,170 --> 00:11:14,850
And why? Your gal pals, Penny and Bernadette,

208
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:17,800
went out shopping for some wedding nonsense without Amy,

209
00:11:17,850 --> 00:11:19,860
an action they took with no thought or regard

210
00:11:19,910 --> 00:11:22,570
as to how it would affect me, the future of string theory

211
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,540
or my Lego fun time!

212
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:28,140
What do you want us to do about it?

213
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:30,360
You clearly weren't listening to my topic sentence:

214
00:11:30,410 --> 00:11:32,480
Get your women in line!

215
00:11:33,190 --> 00:11:35,930
You make them apologize to Amy and set things right.

216
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:38,820
I am a man of science, not someone's snuggle bunny!

217
00:11:39,710 --> 00:11:41,760
Why do I have to talk to Penny?

218
00:11:41,810 --> 00:11:43,070
She's not my girlfriend.

219
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:44,840
You invited her to lunch four years ago.

220
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:47,250
Everything about her is on you; you make it so!

221
00:11:50,790 --> 00:11:52,610
- Moo-shu is Penny. - Thank you.

222
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:53,600
Where's Sheldon?

223
00:11:53,650 --> 00:11:54,870
He was up late last night,

224
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:57,330
so I gave him an early dinner and put him to bed.

225
00:11:58,710 --> 00:11:59,890
That's so sweet.

226
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:02,670
Yeah, but now he's gonna be up at dawn and want to play.

227
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:05,640
So, listen, guys,

228
00:12:05,690 --> 00:12:08,170
the reason he was up late is because he was

229
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:09,580
taking care of Amy.

230
00:12:09,630 --> 00:12:11,240
She's kind of upset.

231
00:12:11,290 --> 00:12:12,070
Why?

232
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:13,760
Her feelings got hurt because you guys

233
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:15,830
went dress shopping without her.

234
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:17,520
I told you that would happen.

235
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:19,820
Okay, look, this is her first time being a bridesmaid

236
00:12:19,870 --> 00:12:21,910
and she's just getting a little crazy with it.

237
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:24,820
She keeps on telling us stories about bridesmaid traditions

238
00:12:24,870 --> 00:12:26,400
in other cultures, and they're all about

239
00:12:26,450 --> 00:12:29,370
getting naked and washing each other.

240
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:38,270
Yeah, and she keeps trying

241
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:40,110
to figure out if our cycles have synced up

242
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:43,690
so we can call ourselves "The three menstra-teers"

243
00:12:44,940 --> 00:12:46,160
We thought it would be easier

244
00:12:46,210 --> 00:12:48,710
to look at dresses one time without her.

245
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:49,970
I feel terrible.

246
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:51,720
I know. Me, too. We'll talk to her.

247
00:12:51,770 --> 00:12:52,500
Hey, wait,

248
00:12:52,550 --> 00:12:54,160
how did she find out?

249
00:12:54,750 --> 00:12:56,830
Raj did it.

250
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:01,970
And he says he would do it again.

251
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:06,550
Hey, I'm sorry.

252
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:09,430
You sent me the picture; I wasn't thinking.

253
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:11,050
- Oh, Howie. - Well, hey, I'm usually

254
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:12,570
pretty good at not blabbing.

255
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:14,260
You tell me tons of stuff about these guys,

256
00:13:14,310 --> 00:13:16,380
 and I never repeat it.

257
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:20,680
- What do you tell him? - Oh,

258
00:13:20,730 --> 00:13:22,180
you know, just pillow talk.

259
00:13:22,230 --> 00:13:24,340
You guys have nothing to worry about.

260
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:26,960
Hold on, did you talk about us when we were dating?

261
00:13:27,010 --> 00:13:29,840
No. All your little secrets are fine.

262
00:13:34,030 --> 00:13:36,150
Yes, absolutely fine.

263
00:13:38,610 --> 00:13:40,450
I'll tell you later.

264
00:13:44,540 --> 00:13:46,440
Come on, tumor.

265
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:51,540
Come on, tumor, Mama needs an aggressive little glioblastoma.

266
00:13:53,130 --> 00:13:55,470
Yay, brain tumor!

267
00:13:56,190 --> 00:14:00,010
Probably not the same reaction you had when you got the news.

268
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:04,160
- Hi. - Hello.

269
00:14:04,210 --> 00:14:05,710
What are you doing here?

270
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:07,920
Well, look, we just wanted to apologize

271
00:14:07,970 --> 00:14:10,130
for not bringing you with us the other day.

272
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:12,960
That's not necessary-- it's like Sesame Street says:

273
00:14:13,010 --> 00:14:14,820
"One of these things is not like the other,

274
00:14:14,870 --> 00:14:17,720
one of these things should die alone."

275
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:21,020
Come on, Amy, let us make it up to you.

276
00:14:21,070 --> 00:14:22,080
We'll have a girl's night;

277
00:14:22,130 --> 00:14:23,280
we'll do whatever you want.

278
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:24,870
We can go down to the Korean baths

279
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:26,400
and do that thing you were talking about

280
00:14:26,450 --> 00:14:28,130
where we wash each other.

281
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:32,280
With-with loofah mitts, no hands.

282
00:14:34,210 --> 00:14:36,600
It's okay. I'm glad this happened.

283
00:14:36,650 --> 00:14:38,870
I can stop pretending that some beautiful girl

284
00:14:38,920 --> 00:14:40,460
and her cute-in-the-right-light friend

285
00:14:40,510 --> 00:14:42,020
want to hang out with me.

286
00:14:42,950 --> 00:14:44,710
Amy, we're really sorry.

287
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:46,220
Yeah, we feel awful.

288
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:48,930
Don't. I'll be okay. You're not the first girls

289
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:51,190
I thought were friends with me who ended up shunning me.

290
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:53,790
It's like elementary school, junior high,

291
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,370
high school, undergrad, grad school,

292
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:58,100
and that semester abroad in Norway all over again.

293
00:14:58,620 --> 00:15:00,380
Oh, come on, Amy.

294
00:15:01,110 --> 00:15:02,600
You don't get it.

295
00:15:03,330 --> 00:15:04,830
Look at this brain.

296
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:07,070
I don't really want to.

297
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:09,590
This is us.

298
00:15:09,640 --> 00:15:11,590
Bernadette, you are the analytical,

299
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:13,240
scientific left hemisphere.

300
00:15:13,290 --> 00:15:16,430
Penny, you're the creative, spontaneous right hemisphere.

301
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:18,100
And where's Amy?

302
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,410
She's right here-- the sad little tumor

303
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:24,120
no one wants to go dress shopping with.

304
00:15:26,790 --> 00:15:28,380
Amy, you're not a tumor.

305
00:15:28,430 --> 00:15:30,640
Penny, tell her she's not a tumor.

306
00:15:41,090 --> 00:15:43,820
Did you know that when Alexander Graham Bell

307
00:15:43,870 --> 00:15:45,520
invented the telephone,

308
00:15:45,570 --> 00:15:48,190
he proposed answering it with "Ahoy"?

309
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:51,660
Ahoy.

310
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,030
I like it.

311
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:58,180
Amy? Is that you?

312
00:15:59,010 --> 00:16:00,920
Have you been drinking?

313
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:04,030
I'm sorry, I bet my sweet what?

314
00:16:05,130 --> 00:16:07,560
Well, all right. We're on our way.

315
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:09,840
What's going on?

316
00:16:09,890 --> 00:16:14,000
It would seem Amy is drunk in a liquor store parking lot.

317
00:16:14,050 --> 00:16:15,430
Really? Amy?

318
00:16:15,570 --> 00:16:17,300
Leonard, be glad you can't keep a woman.

319
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:19,100
They are a handful.

320
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:27,520
Oh, look.

321
00:16:27,570 --> 00:16:29,060
It's Sheldon and little Leonard.

322
00:16:29,110 --> 00:16:30,520
Hi, little Leonard.

323
00:16:32,630 --> 00:16:34,010
Hi, Amy.

324
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:35,470
Hey, Cuddles.

325
00:16:36,370 --> 00:16:37,590
Cuddles?

326
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:39,450
Yes, Cuddles. We cuddled.

327
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:40,990
Grow up, Leonard.

328
00:16:43,290 --> 00:16:44,910
Amy, what are you doing here?

329
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:47,170
I came here to get a bottle of wine

330
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:49,960
like Penny taught me to do when you're sad.

331
00:16:51,350 --> 00:16:53,910
Yeah, but why didn't you go back to your apartment?

332
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:55,530
Didn't you go to high school, Leonard?

333
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:58,450
Parking lots are where all the cool kids hang out.

334
00:16:58,570 --> 00:17:01,810
Not that the rat bastards ever invited me.

335
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:03,790
Maybe we should get you home.

336
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,500
Oh, hang on, hang on, hang on. Sheldon,

337
00:17:06,490 --> 00:17:09,410
what would it take for you to go into that liquor store,

338
00:17:09,460 --> 00:17:10,910
buy a bottle of hooch,

339
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:13,180
take me across the street to that motel,

340
00:17:13,230 --> 00:17:15,040
and have your way with me?

341
00:17:18,740 --> 00:17:21,210
Yeah, Sheldon, what would it take?

342
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:26,950
I'm begging both of you, please, let's go.

343
00:17:27,340 --> 00:17:28,280
Okay.

344
00:17:28,330 --> 00:17:29,350
Come on.

345
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,110
Upsy-daisy.

346
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:33,670
Ooh, finally someone found second base.

347
00:17:38,950 --> 00:17:40,770
I know how you feel.

348
00:17:41,250 --> 00:17:44,440
I got a knife slicing through my frontal lobe, too.

349
00:17:48,570 --> 00:17:49,880
What do you want?

350
00:17:49,930 --> 00:17:52,300
Do you want me to give the friendship bracelet back?

351
00:17:52,350 --> 00:17:54,960
I never gave you a friendship bracelet.

352
00:17:56,340 --> 00:17:58,940
When we first met, I made one and pretended you gave it to me.

353
00:17:58,990 --> 00:18:00,770
You can have it back if you want.

354
00:18:01,990 --> 00:18:05,310
No, you made that for you; I want you to have it.

355
00:18:06,830 --> 00:18:09,530
We know you're upset, and you have every right to be,

356
00:18:09,580 --> 00:18:11,760
but if it's okay with you, we'd like a second chance

357
00:18:11,810 --> 00:18:13,110
to make things right.

358
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:14,660
We are really sorry,

359
00:18:14,710 --> 00:18:16,760
and we were trying to think of some way to show you

360
00:18:16,810 --> 00:18:18,650
how much we care about you.

361
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:19,360
Which is why

362
00:18:19,410 --> 00:18:21,280
it would mean so much if you would agree

363
00:18:21,330 --> 00:18:23,240
to be the maid of honor at my wedding.

364
00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:27,270
What? Wait, is this some kind of practical joke?

365
00:18:27,320 --> 00:18:29,070
Like in Norway, when my "Friends"

366
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:31,300
trapped me in a sauna with a horny otter?

367
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:38,500
No. I-I want you to be the maid of honor.

368
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,180
Oh, my gosh.

369
00:18:40,720 --> 00:18:43,100
No one's every asked me to be the maid of honor before.

370
00:18:43,150 --> 00:18:44,390
Well, that's not true.

371
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:46,650
Once, but then they all died.

372
00:18:48,910 --> 00:18:51,000
So is that a yes?

373
00:18:51,050 --> 00:18:53,340
Yes. Yes.

374
00:18:53,390 --> 00:18:54,400
Oh, my gosh,

375
00:18:54,450 --> 00:18:55,770
I-I have so much to do.

376
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:57,890
There's the bridal shower and the bachelorette party.

377
00:18:57,940 --> 00:18:59,540
What should we do for the bachelorette party?

378
00:18:59,590 --> 00:19:02,560
Oh, I know-- we'll go to a Native American sweat lodge,

379
00:19:02,610 --> 00:19:04,450
we'll take peyote, roll around in the mud,

380
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:06,470
and paint fertility symbols on Bernadette's naked body.

381
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:08,120
So that's happening.

382
00:19:09,370 --> 00:19:10,330
Yeah, sure, sure.

383
00:19:10,380 --> 00:19:11,960
Vegas is fun, too.

384
00:19:12,750 --> 00:19:14,130
I feel like crying.

385
00:19:14,180 --> 00:19:15,700
Of course, I could just be hormonal.

386
00:19:15,750 --> 00:19:18,140
Oh, wait. Maybe our menses are finally syncing up.

387
00:19:18,190 --> 00:19:19,040
Bernadette?

388
00:19:19,090 --> 00:19:20,130
No? Penny?

389
00:19:20,180 --> 00:19:21,800
- Sorry. - Really?

390
00:19:22,010 --> 00:19:22,770
Okay, yeah.

391
00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:23,830
Yay!

392
00:19:30,290 --> 00:19:32,440
Maid of honor Amy Farrah Fowler's

393
00:19:32,490 --> 00:19:34,650
amazing behind-the-scenes wedding video,

394
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:35,820
take one.

395
00:19:38,090 --> 00:19:39,490
We're just trying on dresses--

396
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:41,390
do we really need to record this?

397
00:19:41,970 --> 00:19:44,110
I'm sorry, are you the maid of honor?

398
00:19:46,010 --> 00:19:47,760
I am the bride.

399
00:19:47,810 --> 00:19:49,890
So no. And action.

400
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:53,170
What do you think?

401
00:19:53,220 --> 00:19:54,910
I love it!

402
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:56,300
What are you, a nun?

403
00:19:56,350 --> 00:19:58,180
Come on, bestie, let's see some skin.

404
00:20:01,240 --> 00:20:03,580
Amy! Looking sexy!

405
00:20:03,630 --> 00:20:04,900
You think they don't have mirrors in there?

406
00:20:04,950 --> 00:20:06,400
I know how I look.

407
00:20:13,960 --> 00:20:17,020
Bernadette, you look beautiful!

408
00:20:17,070 --> 00:20:19,360
- You do. - Thank you.

409
00:20:19,410 --> 00:20:21,770
Not Penny beautiful, but beautiful.

410
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:26,090
Come on, bestie, you're up.

411
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:27,550
Give me a minute.

412
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:29,780
What is taking you so long?

413
00:20:30,690 --> 00:20:32,500
Oh, Amy! Get the hell out of here!

414
00:20:32,550 --> 00:20:34,710
- Sorry, sorry. - Oh, God.

415
00:20:34,760 --> 00:20:35,990
- Amy! - Sorry.

